First, find the $x$-coordinate where $y=e$:
$$\begin{align*}
e &= e^{\frac{x}{2}} \\
1 &= \frac{x}{2} \\
x &= 2
\end{align*}$$
Find the derivative of the curve to determine the gradient function:
$$\begin{align*}
y &= e^{\frac{x}{2}} \\
\frac{dy}{dx} &= \frac{1}{2} e^{\frac{x}{2}}
\end{align*}$$
Substitute $x=2$ to find the gradient $m$ at this point:
$$\begin{align*}
m &= \frac{1}{2} e^{\frac{2}{2}} \\
m &= \frac{e}{2}
\end{align*}$$
The gradient relates to the angle of inclination $\theta$ by $m = \tan\theta$:
$$\begin{align*}
\tan\theta &= \frac{e}{2} \\
\theta &= \tan^{-1}\left(\frac{e}{2}\right) \\
\theta &\approx 53.65^{\circ}
\end{align*}$$
Correct to the nearest degree, the angle of inclination is $54^{\circ}$.

