The tangent line $y=mx$ touches the curve $y=\cos x$ at $x=a$. Since the line passes through the origin $(0,0)$ and the point of tangency $(a, \cos a)$, its gradient is:
$$\begin{align*}
m &= \frac{\cos a - 0}{a - 0} = \frac{\cos a}{a}
\end{align*}$$
From the diagram, the $y$-coordinate at $x=a$ is positive and less than $1$ (i.e. $0 < \cos a < 1$). Therefore:
$$\begin{align*}
m &= \frac{\cos a}{a} < \frac{1}{a}
\end{align*}$$
At $x=2\pi$, the $y$-value of the curve is $\cos(2\pi) = 1$.
Between $x = \frac{3\pi}{2}$ and $x = \frac{5\pi}{2}$, the curve $y=\cos x$ is concave down (since $y^{\prime\prime} = -\cos x < 0$ in this interval).
Because the curve is concave down, the tangent line at $x=a$ must lie above the curve for all $x \neq a$ in this interval.
Therefore, at $x=2\pi$, the line $y=mx$ is above the curve $y=\cos x$:
$$\begin{align*}
m(2\pi) &> \cos(2\pi) \\
m(2\pi) &> 1 \\
m &> \frac{1}{2\pi}
\end{align*}$$
Combining these inequalities gives:
$$\begin{align*}
\frac{1}{2\pi} &< m < \frac{1}{a}
\end{align*}$$
